# Caching headers for the Cloudflare Worker's static assets.
#
# THIS FILE IS WHAT SETS THEM. It used to say the opposite — that `_worker.js`
# set these and this was only a fallback — which was the reverse of the truth
# and survived the move off Pages by being read as documentation. wrangler
# parses this file out of the assets directory and the asset router applies it
# ("Parsed 2 valid header rules"), and it does so for every request that matches
# a real file, which is exactly the set of requests `worker/index.js` never
# sees: with `run_worker_first` unset, an asset that exists is served without
# invoking the Worker at all. The immutable branch this file was said to be a
# fallback for was therefore unreachable, and was deleted.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/_expo/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# EVERY response. Unlike its sibling apps this is an ADDITION, not a
# restoration: Noted never sent either header. Cloudflare Pages adds both to
# every response it serves automatically, but only to responses IT builds —
# this app ran `public/_worker.js` in Pages Advanced Mode, which returns
# `env.ASSETS.fetch()` directly and bypasses that injection. Measured on the
# Pages deployment the day it was retired: `noted.oxy.so` sent neither, while
# `oxy-auth.pages.dev`, a plain Pages project with no `_headers` rule for them,
# sent both.
#
# So the move to a Worker took nothing away here, and every other Oxy app that
# left Pages now declares these explicitly. Noted was the last one without them.
#
# `nosniff` is the one that matters: without it a browser may disregard a
# declared Content-Type and sniff the bytes instead, which turns any response
# carrying attacker-influenced content into a possible script.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
